C = [
("h1", "7. “By Him … for Him … Before All Things”"),
("p", "Claim: Colossians 1:16 and 1:17 say three things of Christ that the Scriptures of Israel say of the LORD alone: that all things were created by him, that they were created for him, and that he was before them. Paul says them without a pause and without a defence, and the volume reports them as written."),
("sub", "7.1 The verses"),
("p", "“For by him were all things created, that are in heaven, and that are in earth, visible and invisible, whether they be thrones, or dominions, or principalities, or powers: all things were created by him, and for him: And he is before all things, and by him all things consist” (Colossians 1:16, 17). The sentence is long, and it has a plan. It states the creating, lists the kinds of things created, repeats the creating with a purpose (“for him”), and ends with two statements in the present: he is before all things, and by him all things consist."),
("sub", "7.2 “All things”"),
("p", "The phrase “all things” is explained in the sentence itself: heaven and earth, visible and invisible. Paul adds four words for the powers. The volume does not draw a map of the unseen world from them, and does not say how the four differ. It reports that the list includes them among the things created, and that Colossians 2:10 and 2:15 speak of them again in the letter. John Piper presses the point that the list leaves no exceptions, and the volume agrees with the point and does not go beyond what the list says."),
("sub", "7.3 “By him,” “for him,” “before”"),
("p", "The three are different statements. “By him” names the one through whom the creating was done. “For him” names the one to whom it is directed. “Before all things” names his place in time or rank. A being who is himself among the things created could not be said to be before all of them. The sentence therefore sets him outside the class of the created. The word translated “consist” (Colossians 1:17) names a holding together. Of any thing, it says, he is the reason that it continues."),
("p", "Piper puts the conclusion in a short line: “Christ is the creator of all that is not God.” The volume cites the line as his reading of the sentence. It is an inference from “all things” and not a quotation of Paul. It is, however, the inference the sentence invites, and nothing in the passage resists it."),
("sub", "7.4 Beside 1 Corinthians 8:6"),
("p", "Paul had written the same thing in another form to Corinth. “But to us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we in him; and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him” (1 Corinthians 8:6). The context is meat offered to idols, and “an idol is nothing in the world, and … there is none other God but one” (1 Corinthians 8:4). In the same sentence in which Paul keeps the one God, he places the Lord Jesus Christ beside him, with “all things” coming through him."),
("p", "N. T. Wright calls the verse “possibly the single most revolutionary christological formulation in the whole of early Christianity.” The volume cites him for the weight he gives it and not for his account of its origin. It notes that the verse does not call the Lord Jesus Christ “God” in so many words, and that it places him on the side of the one God and not among the many lords. The two letters say the same thing about creation in different problems."),
("ctbl", "Creation said of Christ in Paul’s letters.", [
    ("The passage", "What it says of Christ and creation", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("1 Corinthians 8:6", "One Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him", "Whether “by whom” names more than agency; the verse says by whom"),
    ("Colossians 1:16", "By him, and for him, all things were created", "How the Father and the Son each have part in the creating"),
    ("Colossians 1:17", "Before all things; by him all things consist", "What “consist” excludes; the verse states it of all things"),
    ("Colossians 2:10", "The head of all principality and power", "Whether the headship is by creation or by something else"),
  ], [0.22, 0.42, 0.36]),
("sub", "7.5 The powers again"),
("p", "The list of thrones, dominions, principalities and powers returns in the letter. “And ye are complete in him, which is the head of all principality and power” (Colossians 2:10). “And having spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (Colossians 2:15). In Ephesians Paul says that God set Christ “at his own right hand in the heavenly places, Far above all principality, and power, and might, and dominion, and every name that is named” (Ephesians 1:20, 21). The same kinds of word occur in the three places, and the volume does not claim that the lists are identical. The point is that wherever Paul lists the powers, Christ is set over them. In Colossians 1:16 the ground of that headship is that they were created by him. In Colossians 2:15 it is that they were spoiled in the Cross, and in Ephesians 1 it is that he sits above them. The chapter takes only the first. The others belong to the volume on the Cross and to the study of the Body’s position."),
("sub", "7.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "“By him,” “for him” and “before all things” are three statements, all made in the sentence.",
    "The list of heaven and earth, visible and invisible, with four words for powers, includes the powers among the things created.",
    "Paul says the same in 1 Corinthians 8:6, where he keeps the one God and places the Lord Jesus Christ beside him.",
    "The sentences do not say how the Father’s part and the Son’s part in creation relate; they say that all things came by him.",
  ]),
("p", "Having said that all things were created by him and for him, Paul goes on to a sentence about what dwells in him. That is the subject of the next chapter."),
]
